---
// Figure: serving the stale copy while one request refreshes it.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
import Readout from '../shared/Readout.astro';
import './stampedes.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="st-stale" title="Serving the stale copy while one request refreshes it" advance={1000} legend={['client', 'hit', 'stale', 'miss', 'write']}
  caption="The same model, at 15 requests a second. Serving stale: the copy counts as fresh for 5 seconds (the soft expiry) and stays in Redis for 15 (the hard expiry). A request that reads a stale copy returns it at once (a triangle) and tries the lock; the winner checks that the copy is still stale and refreshes it in the background.">
  <Choice slot="params" name="mode" label="When the copy is out of date" value="stale"
    options={[{ value: 'lock', label: 'wait for the lock holder' }, { value: 'stale', label: 'serve stale, refresh once' }]} />
  <Fragment slot="stats">
    <Readout name="queries" label="Database queries, latest expiry" kind="database" />
    <Readout name="slowest" label="Slowest response, latest expiry" kind="client" />
    <Readout name="stale" label="Stale responses, latest expiry" kind="stale" />
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>Waiting for the lock holder, each expiry sends one query and the requests that arrive during the rebuild wait up to about 1.5 seconds. Serving stale, each expiry also sends one query, but no request waits for it: the requests that arrive during the refresh, about 20 of them on average, receive the previous copy at once, and the slowest response stays at the 0.2 seconds of an ordinary hit. When the refresh is stored, requests receive the new copy again.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { mountHotKey } from './hotkey-scene.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="st-stale"]').forEach(root => mountHotKey(root));
</script>
